.TH INSTALLER-GXWI 1 "2026-10-06" "Peios"
.SH NAME
installer-gxwi \- the graphical Peios installer, in a browser, as GXWI's overlay
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B installer-gxwi
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-listen
.IR ADDRESS : PORT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
What GXWI runs as its overlay on an install medium: one process for the
machine, which everyone who opens the machine's address in a browser is sent
to, with no logon. It installs nothing itself.
.BR installerd (8)
does that, as SYSTEM, and asks its questions over a socket; this program
holds that conversation, keeps what it has come to as one state, and serves a
page that draws it. Every browser is sent the same thing, and sent it again
whenever it changes, so two people looking see the same disk chosen and are
moved on together.
.PP
The page goes from what to do with the machine, through which disk and
whether it is really to be erased, to the installation as it runs and the
restart. The button that begins an installation is held down rather than
pressed. A job that fails ends the conversation; what it ended with stays on
the page until someone asks to start again. A job that finishes goes on to
.BR installerd 's
offer to restart the machine, and the page waits for the machine to come back
and goes where it lands: first-boot setup, Peios, or the installer again.
.PP
GXWI starts it, as the account its
.I OverlayUsername
names, and hands it the listening socket it serves on. The medium makes it the
overlay, in
.IR Machine\eSoftware\eGXWI ;
the package does not, so an installed machine that carries it is not an
installer. Started by hand, it has no socket and says so.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " PATH"
.BR installerd 's
socket.
The default is
.IR /run/installerd.sock .
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " ADDRESS" : PORT
Serve here instead of on a socket handed over, for working on the page where
there is no GXWI.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of these.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR oobe-gxwi (1),
.BR installerd (8),
.BR gxwid (1)
